# ---------------- 11.1 ----------------
dict(pas="11.1", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^先/(those who) first | 進/came forward | 於/in | 禮/the rites | 樂，=yuè/(and) music | ^野/rustic | 人/men | 也；/(they are) | ^後/(those who) later | 進/came forward | 於/in | 禮/the rites | 樂，=yuè/(and) music | ^君子/gentlemen | 也。」/(they are)",
  l="The Master said: “Those who came forward first in the rites and music —\nmen of the countryside;\nthose who came forward later in the rites and music —\ngentlemen.”",
  i="The Master said: “The men of the earlier generation, in the rites and music, are counted rustics; the men of the later one are counted gentlemen.”",
  n="The book is named 先進 from its opening words. Zhu Xi's heading: much of it weighs the disciples, worthy or not, in twenty-five chapters (He Yan counts twenty-three); he quotes Hu Yin: four passages record Min Ziqian's words and deeds, and one of them calls him simply 'Master Min' (閔子, 11.12), so perhaps Min's own followers wrote it down. The two halves of this line are the same sentence twice: a topic ('those who came forward first in the rites and music'), then a noun predicate closed by 也 — 'X, (they are) Y'. 於 is 'in, in respect of'. 樂 is yuè 'music' here; 禮樂 'the rites and music' together are the whole of cultivated form. Zhu Xi: 先進 and 後進 are 'the earlier and the later generation' (前輩後輩); 野人 are the people out beyond the city walls, 君子 worthy officers and grandees. On his reading the Master is reporting how people of his day talk. He quotes Master Cheng: the earlier men had pattern and substance in due measure in the rites and music, yet people now call them plain and rustic; the later men's pattern outruns their substance, yet people now call them polished, gentlemen — at the end of the Zhou pattern had the upper hand, so men spoke so without seeing their own excess. He Yan's commentary takes the two verdicts more at face value: 先進 and 後進 are earlier and later generations of officers; the rites and music change with the age; the later men have the mean that suits their time, and so are gentlemen, while the earlier keep the old manner, and so are rustics."),
dict(w="如/if | 用/(I were to) use | 之，/them | ^則/then | 吾/I | 從=cóng/(would) follow | 先/(those who came) first | 進。/forward",
  l="If I were to use them,\nthen I would follow those who came forward first.",
  i="But if it were for me to put them to use, I would go with the earlier men.",
  n="如 'if' opens a condition and 則 'then' answers it: the frame you will meet everywhere. 用之 'use them' — Zhu Xi: use the rites and music. 從 cóng 'to follow, go with'. The speech runs on past Zhu Xi's comment; this continuation is left unquoted. Zhu Xi: having given what people of the day said, the Master states where he himself stands, wishing to cut back what is excessive and come to the mean. He Yan's Bao Xian: he would change the customs of the age and bring them back to plainness; and the commentary adds that the earlier men were nearer the old ways, so he followed them."),
# ---------------- 11.2 ----------------
dict(pas="11.2", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^從=cóng/(those who) followed | 我/me | 於/in | 陳、/Chen | 蔡/(and) Cai | 者，/the ones who | ^皆/all | 不/(do) not | 及/reach | 門/(my) gate | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “Those who followed me in Chen and Cai,\nall of them, do not reach my gate.”",
  i="The Master said: “The men who went through Chen and Cai with me — not one of them is at my door now.”",
  n="陳 Chen and 蔡 Cai were small states south of Lu, where, tradition says, the Master and his followers were cut off and ran short of food. 從我…者 'the ones who followed me…': 者 closes the whole phrase into a noun. Zhu Xi marks this 從 去聲, the reading (zòng) for going along as an attendant; modern practice reads cóng, and so do we here and in 11.9. 皆 'all, without exception'. 不及門 'do not reach the gate'. Zhu Xi: the Master was once in straits between Chen and Cai, and many disciples were with him; now none of them was at his gate, so he thought of them — he did not forget those who had shared his hardship. He Yan's Zheng Xuan reads it another way: those who followed him in his straits all failed to reach 'the gate of office' (仕進之門) and missed their due place. Variant: He Yan's text reads 皆不及門者也, 'were all ones who did not reach the gate'."),
dict(w="^徳=dé/virtuous | 行：=xíng/conduct | 顔淵、=yán yuān/Yan Yuan | 閔子騫、=mǐn zǐ qiān/Min Ziqian | 冉伯牛、=rǎn bó niú/Ran Bo Niu — the disciple Ran Geng//Ran (a surname)//Bo (eldest)//Niu (ox) | 仲弓。=zhòng gōng/Zhonggong | ^言/speech | 語：=yǔ/(and) words | 宰我、=zǎi wǒ/Zai Wo | 子貢。=zǐ gòng/Zigong | ^政/government | 事：/affairs | 冉有、=rǎn yǒu/Ran You | 季路。=jì lù/Ji Lu | ^文/letters | 學：/(and) learning | 子游、=zǐ yóu/Ziyou | 子夏。=zǐ xià/Zixia",
  l="Virtuous conduct: Yan Yuan, Min Ziqian, Ran Bo Niu, Zhonggong.\nSpeech: Zai Wo, Zigong.\nThe affairs of government: Ran You, Ji Lu.\nLetters and learning: Ziyou, Zixia.",
  i="For virtuous conduct there were Yan Yuan, Min Ziqian, Ran Bo Niu and Zhonggong; for speaking, Zai Wo and Zigong; for the business of government, Ran You and Ji Lu; for letters and learning, Ziyou and Zixia.",
  n="A bare list: each heading names a field, then the men who excelled in it. 行 is 'conduct' here; Zhu Xi marks it 去聲 (xìng, the noun), but modern practice reads xíng and so do we. 語 yǔ 'words'. The ten are familiar from earlier books: Yan Yuan is Yan Hui; Min Ziqian is Min Sun (6.7); Ran Bo Niu is Ran Geng, the Bo Niu of 6.8; Zhonggong is Ran Yong; Zai Wo is Zai Yu; Zigong is Duanmu Ci; Ran You is Ran Qiu; Ji Lu is Zilu, Zhong You; Ziyou is Yan Yan; Zixia is Bu Shang. 文學 is not 'literature' in the modern sense but letters and learning: knowledge of the Odes, the Documents and the rites. Zhu Xi: the disciples, prompted by the Master's words above, recorded these ten and listed each one's strength under four heads (the 'four branches', 四科) — which shows the Master taught each man according to his gifts. He quotes Master Cheng: the four branches name only those who were with the Master in Chen and Cai; the worthy disciples were far more than these — Master Zeng, who handed on the Way, is not among them — so the talk of 'the ten wise ones' (十哲) is a popular notion. Variant: He Yan's text puts 子曰 'the Master said' before the list, making it the Master's own words rather than the recorders'."),
# ---------------- 11.3 ----------------
dict(pas="11.3", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^回/Hui | 也，/(as for) | ^非/(is) not | 助=zhù/helps | 我/me | 者/one who | 也，/(assertion) | ^於/with | 吾/my | 言/words | 無/(there is) nothing | 所/that | 不/(he is) not | 說。」=yuè/pleased with",
  l="The Master said: “Hui —\nhe is not one who helps me;\nwith my words, there is nothing he is not pleased by.”",
  i="The Master said: “Hui does nothing to help me along. Whatever I say, it all pleases him.”",
  n="回也 'Hui —': a name plus 也 sets up the topic. 非…者也 'is not one who…'. 於吾言 'with respect to my words'. 無所不說 'there is nothing that he is not pleased with': a double negative, 'everything pleases him'. 說 is yuè 'pleased' (Zhu Xi: 音悅). Zhu Xi: 'helping me' is what Zixia did in 3.8 ('the one who stirs me up is Shang') — by doubts and questions teacher and pupil grow together; Yan Hui took in the sage's words silently and understood them in his heart, with nothing to ask, so the Master spoke thus. The words sound almost regretful, but in truth he is deeply pleased. Hu Yin: did the Master really look to Hui for help? It is the sage's modesty, and deep praise of Yan Hui besides. He Yan's Kong Anguo: 助 is 'to add to'; Hui understood as soon as he heard, so there was nothing to set the Master off or add to his thought (無可發起增益)."),
