dict(pas="11.4", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^孝/filial | 哉，/how | 閔子騫！=mǐn zǐ qiān/Min Ziqian | ^人/others | 不/do not | 閒=jiàn/set (anything) against | 於/(what is said) by | 其/his | 父母/father and mother | 昆弟=kūn dì/(and) brothers//elder brother//younger brother | 之/(genitive) | 言。」/words",
  l="The Master said: “How filial he is, Min Ziqian!\nOthers set nothing against the words of his father, mother and brothers.”",
  i="The Master said: “What a good son Min Ziqian is! When his parents and brothers speak well of him, no one else has a word to say against it.”",
  n="孝哉閔子騫 puts the predicate first for an exclamation, 'filial — how! — Min Ziqian' (as 9.2 大哉孔子). 閒 jiàn (Zhu Xi: 去聲) is 'a gap', and as a verb 'to find a gap in, to set against': others find no gap between what they say of him and what his own family says. 昆 is an elder brother; 昆弟 'brothers'. Zhu Xi quotes Hu Yin: his parents and brothers praised his filial and brotherly conduct, and everyone believed them, with no word to the contrary; for the reality of it had built up within and showed without, and so the Master sighed and praised him. Min Ziqian is the disciple Min Sun (6.7)."),
# ---------------- 11.5 ----------------
dict(pas="11.5", w="南容/Nan Rong | 三/three times | 復/went over | 白=bái/(the lines on) the white | 圭，/jade tablet | ^孔子/Kongzi | 以/took | 其/his | 兄/elder brother | 之/'s | 子/child — a daughter | 妻=qì/(and) gave (her) as wife | 之。/to him",
  l="Nan Rong three times went over the white jade tablet;\nKongzi gave him his elder brother's daughter as wife.",
  i="Nan Rong would repeat the lines about the white jade tablet three times over, and Kongzi gave him his elder brother's daughter in marriage.",
  n="Nan Rong is the disciple of 5.1, and the second half of the line repeats 5.1 word for word: 以其兄之子妻之 'taking his elder brother's child, he gave her to him as wife', with 妻 read qì, the verb (Zhu Xi: 去聲). Zhu Xi also marks 三 去聲, a reading for 'over and over'; we print sān, as at 5.19. 白圭 points to an ode in the Major Elegantiae (大雅, the ode 抑), which Zhu Xi quotes: 'A flaw in a white jade tablet can still be ground away; a flaw in these words cannot be mended.' Nan Rong, he says, repeated these lines three times a day (the story is in the Kongzi jiayu) — his heart was set on care in speech, which is why, as 5.1 says, he would not be passed over in a well-governed state nor come to harm in a badly governed one; and so Kongzi gave him his brother's daughter. He quotes Fan Zuyu: words are the outward show of conduct and conduct the substance of words; no one who speaks lightly is careful in what he does, and one so careful of his words must be careful in his deeds too."),
# ---------------- 11.6 ----------------
dict(pas="11.6", w="季康子=jì kāng zǐ/Ji Kangzi | 問：「/asked | ^弟子=dì zǐ/(among your) disciples | 孰/who | 為=wéi/is | 好=hào/fond of | 學？」/learning | ^孔子/Kongzi | 對/answered | 曰：「/saying | ^有/there was | 顔回=yán huí/Yan Hui//Yan (a surname; the edition's form of 顏)//Hui (his personal name) | 者/one | 好=hào/fond of | 學，/learning | ^不/(it was) not | 幸/(his) luck — unluckily | 短/short | 命/(his) allotted life | 死/(he has) died | 矣，/(change of state) | ^今/now | 也/(pause) | 則/then | 亡。」=wú/there is none (= 無)",
  l="Ji Kangzi asked: “Of your disciples, who is fond of learning?”\nKongzi answered, saying: “There was Yan Hui, who was fond of learning;\nunluckily he was short-lived, and he has died.\nNow there is none.”",
  i="Ji Kangzi asked which of the disciples loved learning. Kongzi answered: “There was Yan Hui. He loved learning. Sadly he was not given long, and he is dead. Now there is no one.”",
  n="Duke Ai asked the same question in the same words in 6.2, and the answer here is that one shortened: it leaves out 'he did not shift his anger, he did not repeat a fault' and the closing 'I have not heard of one fond of learning'. 季康子 Ji Kangzi, head of the Ji house (2.20), is the asker. 孰為好學 'who is fond of learning', 為 wéi 'is', 好 hào (Zhu Xi: 去聲). 亡 is wú, the same word as 無. Zhu Xi quotes Fan Zuyu on why the answers differ in fullness: a minister answering his ruler must tell him everything; one like Kangzi must wait until he can ask properly, and only then be told — this is the way of teaching. Variant: He Yan's text gives the answer at the full length of 6.2 (不遷怒，不貳過 … 未聞好學者也)."),
# ---------------- 11.7 ----------------
dict(pas="11.7", w="顔淵=yán yuān/Yan Yuan | 死，/died | ^顔路=yán lù/Yan Lu — Yan Yuan's father//Yan (a surname)//Lu (his courtesy name) | 請/asked for | 子/the Master | 之/'s | 車=chē/carriage | 以/so as to | 為=wéi/make | 之/for him | 椁。=guǒ/an outer coffin",
  l="Yan Yuan died;\nYan Lu asked for the Master's carriage, to make an outer coffin for him.",
  i="When Yan Yuan died, his father Yan Lu asked for the Master's carriage, so that he could provide an outer coffin for his son.",
  n="Zhu Xi: Yan Lu was Yan Yuan's father, personal name Wuyou 無繇; he was six years younger than Confucius and studied with him when the Master first began to teach. 椁 guǒ (the edition's form of 槨) is the outer coffin, the wooden casing set round the coffin itself. 以為之椁 'thereby to make him an outer coffin': 以 'so as to', 為 wéi 'to make', 之 'for him'. Zhu Xi: he asked for the carriage meaning to sell it and buy the outer coffin; He Yan's Kong Anguo says the same, adding that the family was poor. Variant: He Yan's text lacks 以為之椁 — Yan Lu simply 'asked for the Master's carriage'."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^才/talented | 不/(or) not | 才，/talented | ^亦/still | 各/each | 言/speaks of | 其/his | 子/son | 也。/(assertion) | ^鯉=lǐ/Li | 也/(as for) | 死，/(when he) died | ^有/there was | 棺=guān/a coffin | 而/but | 無/no | 椁。=guǒ/outer coffin | ^吾/I | 不/did not | 徒=tú/on foot | 行=xíng/go | 以/so as to | 為=wéi/make | 之/for him | 椁。=guǒ/an outer coffin | ^以/because | 吾/I | 從=cóng/follow | 大夫=dà fū/the grandees | 之/'s | 後，/rear | ^不/(I) may not | 可/(be allowed to) | 徒=tú/on foot | 行=xíng/go | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “Talented or not talented,\neach still speaks of his own son.\nWhen Li died,\nthere was a coffin and no outer coffin.\nI did not go on foot to make him an outer coffin.\nBecause I follow at the rear of the grandees,\nI may not go on foot.”",
  i="The Master said: “Gifted or not, each of us is speaking of his own son. When Li died he had a coffin but no outer coffin; I did not take to walking to give him one. I still have a place, at the back, among the grandees, and it would not do for me to go about on foot.”",
  n="才不才 'talented, not talented' — whether a son is gifted or not. 亦 'still, all the same'. 鯉 Li is Kong Li, the Master's son, courtesy name Boyu, who died before his father (Zhu Xi; He Yan's Kong Anguo). 有棺而無椁 'there was a coffin but no outer coffin'. 徒行 'to go on foot', 徒 tú 'on foot'. 以吾從大夫之後 'because I follow at the rear of the grandees': 以 'because'. Zhu Xi: Li's gifts did not match Yan Yuan's, but to the Master and to Yan Lu, each looking as a father, both were sons. The Master had by then retired from office, but still took his place in the ranks of the grandees; 'at the rear' is modest. He quotes Hu Yin at length: the Master once unharnessed an outside horse as a funeral gift to a former host (a story in the Book of Rites) — why refuse Yan Lu now? A burial can do without an outer coffin, and a horse can be given away and bought again; but a grandee may not go on foot, and the carriage his ruler conferred may not be given to another to be sold in the market; besides, straining to meet the wishes of a poor acquaintance would be neither sincere nor straight. Some say a gentleman performs the rites as his means allow; but the gentleman spends according to what is right, not merely according to what he has. Variants: He Yan's text reads 鯉死 (without 也), 吾不可徒行 'I could not go on foot', and 以吾從大夫之後，吾以不可徒行也."),
